        // Method 5 - Optimal Solution.
        // Approach:
        // - Instead of using separate prefix and postfix arrays,
        //   reuse the output array to store prefix products.
        // - First traversal:
        //      Build the prefix product directly into the result array.
        // - Second traversal (from right to left):
        //      Maintain a running postfix product.
        //      Multiply each prefix product with the postfix product.
        // - This eliminates the need for separate prefix and postfix arrays,
        //   reducing the extra space usage.
        
        // Time Complexity: O(n)
        // - One traversal computes the prefix products.
        // - One traversal computes the postfix products
        //   while updating the result.
        // - O(2n) simplifies to O(n).
        
        // Space Complexity: O(1)
        // - No additional arrays are created except the required output array.
        // - Only two integer variables (pre and post) are used.
        // - Since the output array is not counted as extra space
        //   (it is required for the answer),
        //   the extra space complexity is O(1).
        int resultarr[] = new int[nums.length];
        int pre = 1;
        int post = 1;
        for(int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++){
            resultarr[i] = pre;
            pre*= nums[i];
        }
        for(int i = nums.length-1; i >=0; i--){
            resultarr[i] = resultarr[i] * post;
            post*= nums[i];
        }
        return resultarr;
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
        
